Speech at controlled noise levels, with the reference implementation's output beside it.

    in_SNR5_sp01.wav   sp01 mixed with noise at 5 dB SNR
    out_SNR5_sp01.wav  what mmse_log_spu.py makes of it
    in_SNR15_sp01.wav  the same at 15 dB
    out_SNR15_sp01.wav

The four files came from github.com/vipchengrui/traditional-speech-enhancement, whose code is MIT
and which follows Loizou, Speech Enhancement: Theory and Practice. Mono, 16-bit, 8 kHz.

Licensing, plainly: the MIT license on that repository covers its code. It says nothing about these
audio files, and the files carry no metadata. The name sp01 at 5 and 15 dB SNR, 8 kHz mono, is the
naming of NOIZEUS (Philipos C. Loizou, UT Dallas, https://ecs.utdallas.edu/loizou/speech/noizeus/),
a corpus published for research with a request to cite Hu and Loizou (2007) and no redistribution
terms stated anywhere we can find. So we cannot say these are ours to redistribute.

They are here only because the denoiser has to be checked against the reference implementation on
the same input. If that is not good enough, the fix is to generate our own: any speech recording we
own, mixed with noise at those two SNRs, run through mmse_log_spu.py to make the out_ files.
